+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service, store or dependency for larder, or weighs options in a decision record. It states
+what the team favors when options conflict. Written after the same argument came up a third time: Redis for sessions
+in 2023, Redis for Celery's queue in 2025 and Elasticsearch for pantry item search in October, in
+[issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), each proposal rediscovering the rule from scratch.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Run one database and one web app
+
+Larder runs one Postgres database and one web app, with its worker; a new service has to do something Postgres
+cannot, and two volunteers must be able to restore it.
+
+- **Why**: larder is run by volunteers, and a service only one of them can restore fails when that one is away. In
+  March 2025 the Redis instance holding Celery's queue evicted 300 receipt jobs on a Saturday night, and the one
+  volunteer who could restore it spent a day resending receipts by hand. Each time the question came up, Postgres did
+  the job.
+- **Rules out**: a self-run service added for what Postgres already does, such as Redis for sessions or for a job
+  queue, or Elasticsearch for search that Postgres full-text search serves; and any self-run service fewer than two
+  volunteers can restore.
+- **Does not rule out**: a service larder does not run, such as the external card gateway or a hosted service nobody
+  has to operate; or a self-run service that does what Postgres cannot and that two volunteers can restore.
+- **A proposal starts from**: what Postgres cannot do here, and which two volunteers will restore the service.
+- **Applied in**: [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md),
+  [run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
